he other hand, if there are hardly any basic moves, the number of conﬁguration\\ntypes is always independent of the actual moves.\\nthe electronic journal of combinatorics 21(3) (2014), #P3.33\\n18--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA q-Queens Problem. I. General Theory (https://www.academia.edu/76124094/A_q_Queens_Problem_I_General_Theory)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Based on the (apparent) facts about queens and nightriders (see below), we propose: Conjecture 5.7. ... Take the basic moves as coordinate vectors in a new coordinate system, in which m1 is the horizontal move to the right and m2 is the vertical move up. ... We noticed that three queens and three nightriders have the same number of combinatorial types of nonattacking configuration. The queen and nightrider also have the same number of moves.\\n\\u0003 20 SETH CHAIKEN, CHRISTOPHER R. H. HANUSA, AND THOMAS ZASLAVSKY Thus, for any piece P, the formula for uP (q; n), upon setting n = −1, must yield 1 when q = 1 and |M| when q = 2. This provides a means for checking formulas. Based on the (apparent) facts about queens and nightriders (see below), we propose: Conjecture 5.7. The number of nonattacking configuration types of 3 pieces depends only on |M|. We think the number depends on the actual moves for large q, though we are not sure where that dependence begins; we suspect q = 4 or q = 5. On the other hand, if there are hardly any basic moves, the number of configuration types is always independent of the actual moves. Theorem 5.8. The number of unlabelled combinatorial types of nonattacking configuration of q pieces is 1 for a piece with only one move and q! for any piece with exactly two moves. Proof. The case |M| = 1 is obvious. Assume M = {m1 , m2 } and label the pieces. Take the basic moves as coordinate vectors in a new coordinate system, in which m1 is the horizontal move to the right and m2 is the vertical move up.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA q-QUEENS PROBLEM (https://people.math.binghamton.edu/zaslav/Tpapers/qqs1.pdf)\\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Combinatorial conﬁguration types. {combconﬁgtypes} [Date added: 24 Jan ... We noticed that three queens and three nightriders have the same number of combinatorial ... The queen and nightrider also have the same number ... The slope matroid SMq(P){d:SM} of order q of a piece P with basic move set (or for this\\nA q-QUEENS PROBLEM\\nI. GENERAL THEORY\\nAugust 7, 2014\\n25\\n7. Questions, Extensions\\n{last}\\nWork on nonattacking chess placements raises many questions, several of which have\\ngeneral interest. We propose the following questions and directions, with others to come in\\nsubsequent parts of this series.\\n7.1. Combinatorial conﬁguration types. {combconﬁgtypes} [Date added: 24 Jan\\n2014.]\\nWe noticed that three queens and three nightriders have the same number of combinatorial\\ntypes of nonattacking conﬁguration. The queen and nightrider also have the same number\\nof moves.\\nQuestion 7.1. {Q:combconﬁgtypes} Does the number of combinatorial types of nonattack-\\ning conﬁguration depend only on the number of moves? If so, what is the formula?\\n7.2. The number of symmetry types. {sym}\\nWe count nonattacking conﬁgurations assuming the board has a ﬁxed orientation. If we\\nconsidered two conﬁgurations to be the same if they are equivalent under symmetries of\\nthe board, the counting problem changes. Asymptotically one would expect the number of\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA q-QUEENS PROBLEM (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=93f38693762461b2b739fb695da19209d9f4e45e&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; queens to three nightriders in the end of Section I.5. ... Conjecture 4.4. The number of combinatorial conﬁguration types of three pieces is\\n22\\nChaiken, Hanusa, and Zaslavsky\\nProof. Set n = −1 in uQhk(q; n) and apply Theorem I.5.3.\\n□\\nFor q = 2 we get the number of basic moves, in accord with Proposition I.5.6. For q = 3\\nthe number of types depends only on the number of moves, just as when we compared three\\nqueens to three nightriders in the end of Section I.5.\\nThe numbers match [4, Sequence\\nA084990], whose formula is s(s2 + 3s −1)/3 with s := |M|.\\nConjecture 4.4. The number of combinatorial conﬁguration types of three pieces is\\n|M|\\n\\u0000\\n|M|2 + 3|M| −1\\n\\u0001\\n/3.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA q-QUEENS PROBLEM (https://people.math.binghamton.edu/zaslav/Tpapers/qqs4.pdf)\\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; A q-Queens Problem. ... q (Theorem 5.6); and if Conjecture 2.4 is true, the period of its counting quasipolynomial ... For q = 3, a triangular conﬁguration of three pieces on the square board,\\nA q-Queens Problem. IV. Attacking Configurations and Their Denominators\\n11\\n5. Pieces with Three or More Moves\\nFrom now on we assume the square board.\\nWith three or more moves, three new conﬁgurations appear: a triangle of pairwise at-\\ntacking pieces (Section 5.1), a golden parallelogram (Example 5.4), and with four moves, a\\ntwisted Fibonacci spiral (Example 6.6). The latter two, which combine 2q −3 move equa-\\ntions and three ﬁxations, yield the largest vertex denominators known to us (see especially\\nSection 5.2).\\nA piece with at least three moves has not only new conﬁguration types; it also enters a\\nnew domain of complexity. There is not a straightforward generalization of Theorem 4.1\\ninvolving rigid cycles, like that in Figure 5.1(a), whose points are all on the boundary of\\nthe board. We now see rigid cycles of a new kind, as in Figure 5.1(b), whose points are not\\nall boundary points. We also lose constancy of the denominator in a very strong way. For\\nevery piece with at least three moves, the denominator grows exponentially or faster with\\nq (Theorem 5.6); and if Conjecture 2.4 is true, the period of its counting quasipolynomial\\ngrows as fast.\\n5.1. Triangle conﬁgurations.\\nWith three (or more) moves, a new key conﬁguration appears: a triangle of pairwise\\nattacking pieces, whose denominator we can calculate exactly.\\nConsider a piece with the three moves m1 = (c1, d1), m2 = (c2, d2), and m3 = (c3, d3).\\nSince no move is a multiple of another, there exist nonzero integers w1, w2, and w3 with\\ngcd(w1, w2, w3) = 1 such that w1m1 + w2m2 + w3m3 = (0, 0). The wi are unique up to\\nnegating them all.\\nProposition 5.1. For q = 3, a triangular conﬁguration of three pieces on the square board,\\nattacking pairwise along three distinct move directions m1 = (c1, d1), m2 = (c2, d2), and\\nm3 = (c3, d3), together with three ﬁxations that ﬁx its position in the square [0, 1]2, gives a\\nvertex z of the inside-out polytope. Its denominator is\\n(5.1)\\n∆(z) = max(|w1c1|, |w1d1|, |w2c2|, |w2d2|, |w3c3|, |w3d3|).\\nThe pieces may be at corners, and there may be two pieces on the same edge. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA q-Queens Problem IV. Attacking configurations and their denominators (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2019.111649)\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Two three-move riders (the trident and semiqueen) and a four-move rider (the queen). ... They also serve to locate the configuration in the unit-square board, by giving the unique positive integer 𝑁 such that dividing by 𝑁 fits the shrunken configuration 𝐳 into the square board with three queens fixed on its boundary; thus 𝐳 is a vertex with denominator 𝛥⁡(𝐳)=𝑁 (see Lemma 2.1). ... Conjecture 5.5 specializes to:\\nThese fixations define the smallest square box that contains all pieces in the configuration. They also serve to locate the configuration in the unit-square board, by giving the unique positive integer 𝑁 such that dividing by 𝑁 fits the shrunken configuration 𝐳 into the square board with three queens fixed on its boundary; thus 𝐳 is a vertex with denominator 𝛥⁡(𝐳)=𝑁 (see Lemma 2.1).\\n\\nFig. 5.2(a) shows the golden rectangle configuration of 12 semiqueens; it fits in an 8 × 13 rectangle. Fig. 5.2(b) is a configuration that has the same denominator and is similarly related to a discrete Fibonacci spiral (which will be explained in Section 6, where it figures more prominently).\\n\\nIt is straightforward to find the coordinates of ℙ𝑖 in the golden rectangle configuration, which we present without proof. We assume coordinates with origin in the lower left corner of Fig. 5.2(a).\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (242KB)\\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\\n\\nFig. 5.2. (a) The golden rectangle configuration. (b) A configuration based on a discrete Fibonacci spiral.\\n\\nProposition 5.3\\n\\nFor the semiqueen ℙ=ℚ21, when the pieces are arranged in the golden rectangle configuration, ℙ1 is in position (1,0) and, for 𝑖≥2, ℙ𝑖 is in position (𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋,0)if  𝑖≡0⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1)if  𝑖≡1⁢mod⁡4,(0,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋)if  𝑖≡2⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋)if  𝑖≡3⁢mod⁡4. The step from ℙ𝑖−1 to ℙ𝑖 is 𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1⁡(1,−1)if  𝑖≡0⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1⁡(0,1)if  𝑖≡1⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1⁡(−1,1)if  𝑖≡2⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1⁡(1,0)if  𝑖≡3⁢mod⁡4.\\n\\nTheorem 5.6\\n\\nThe denominators 𝐷𝑞⁡(ℙ) of any piece that has three or more moves increase at least exponentially in 𝑞. Specifically, they are bounded below by 12⁢𝜑𝑞/2 when 𝑞≥12, where 𝜑 is the golden ratio.\\n\\nProof\\n\\nTo prove the theorem it suffices to produce a vertex of ([0,1]2⁢𝑞,𝒜𝑞ℙ) with denominator exceeding 𝜑𝑞/2.\\n\\nFirst consider the semiqueen. The points ℚ211 and ℚ214⁢𝑗 of the golden rectangle have coordinates (1,0) and (𝐹2⁢𝑗,0). Letting 𝑞=4⁢𝑗 or 4⁢𝑗+1 gives an 𝑥-difference of 𝐹2⁢𝑗−1 for a golden rectangle of 𝑞 pieces. Similarly, letting 𝑞=4⁢𝑗+2 or 4⁢𝑗+3 gives a 𝑦-difference of 𝐹2⁢𝑗+1. The golden rectangle is a vertex configuration so it follows by Lemma 2.1 that the vertex 𝐳 has 𝛥⁡(𝐳)≥𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋−1. A calculation shows that 𝐹⌊𝑗⌋−1>12⁢𝜑𝑗+12 for 𝑗≥6. The theorem for ℚ21 follows.\\n\\nAn arbitrary piece with three (or more) moves has a golden parallelogram configuration formed from the golden rectangle by the linear transformation (1,0)↦𝑤1⁢𝑚1 and (0,1)↦𝑤2⁢𝑚2. We may choose these moves from at least three, so we can select 𝑚1 to have 𝑐1≠0 and 𝑚2 to have 𝑑2≠0. The displacement from ℚ211 to ℚ214⁢𝑗 becomes that from ℙ1 at 𝑤1⁢𝑚1 to ℙ4⁢𝑗 at 𝐹2⁢𝑗⁡𝑤1⁢𝑚1. This displacement is (𝐹2⁢𝑗−1)⁢(𝑤1⁢𝑐1,𝑤1⁢𝑑1). Since 𝑐1≠0, the 𝑥-displacement is at least that for ℚ21; therefore the denominator of the corresponding vertex for ℙ is bounded below by 𝐹2⁢𝑗−1, just as it is for ℚ21. Similarly, the 𝑦-displacement for 𝑞=4⁢𝑗+2 is bounded below by 𝐹2⁢𝑗+1. This reduces the problem to the semiqueen, which is solved.  □\\n\\nWe know from Proposition 2.2 that 𝐷𝑞⁡(ℙ) is weakly increasing. If, as we believe, the period equals 𝐷𝑞⁡(ℙ), then the period increases at least exponentially for any piece with more than two moves.\\n\\nExample 5.7\\n\\nThe semiqueen has (up to symmetry) only one other golden parallelogram besides the golden rectangle; it is shown in Fig. 5.4. It has a larger denominator than the golden rectangle configuration when 𝑞 is odd and 𝑞≥7. If we put ℙ2 in position (0,0), then ℙ1 is in position (0,−1) and for 𝑖≥2, ℙ𝑖 is in position (𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1,−𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋)if 𝑖≡0⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋+1−1,−𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋)if 𝑖≡1⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1,0)if 𝑖≡2⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋+1−1,−𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1)if 𝑖≡3⁢mod⁡4. Conjecture 5.5 specializes to:\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (104KB)\\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\\n\\nFig. 5.4. A golden parallelogram configuration of 𝑞 semiqueens has the largest denominator when 𝑞 is odd.\\n\\nConjecture 5.8\\n\\nThe largest denominator of a vertex for 𝑞 semiqueens ℚ21 is 𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋ if 𝑞 is even and 𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+1−1 if 𝑞 is odd.\\n\\nExample 5.9\\n\\nThe trident ℚ12 has move set 𝐌={(0,1),(1,1),(−1,1)}. It gives the three distinct golden parallelogram configurations shown in Fig. 5.5. The piece positions for 𝑖≥2 for the configuration in Fig. 5.5(a) are (0,2⁢𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1)if 𝑖≡0⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋+2−1)if 𝑖≡1⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1)if 𝑖≡2⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋+1+𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1−1)if 𝑖≡3⁢mod⁡4, so the largest denominator for such a configuration is 2⁢𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋−1if 𝑞≡0⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+2−1if 𝑞≡1⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+1−1if 𝑞≡2⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+1+𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋−1−1if 𝑞≡3⁢mod⁡4. On the other hand, in Fig. 5.5(c), the piece positions for 𝑖≥2 are (𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1)if 𝑖≡0⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋+1+𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1−1)if 𝑖≡1⁢mod⁡4,(0,2⁢𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1)if 𝑖≡2⁢mod⁡4,(𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋−1,𝐹⌊𝑖/2⌋+2−1)if 𝑖≡3⁢mod⁡4, which yields a largest denominator of such a configuration of 𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+1−1if 𝑞≡0⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+1+𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋−1−1if 𝑞≡1⁢mod⁡4,2⁢𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋−1if 𝑞≡2⁢mod⁡4,𝐹⌊𝑞/2⌋+2−1if 𝑞≡3⁢mod⁡4. Conjecture 5.5 specializes to:\\n\\nImage\\n\\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (219KB)\\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\\n\\nFig. 5.5. The three golden parallelograms for the trident ℚ12. For twelve pieces, the denominators are 25, 21, and 20, respectively.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA $q$-Queens Problem. IV. Attacking Configurations and Their Denominators (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326366928_A_q-Queens_Problem_IV_Attacking_Configurations_and_Their_Denominators/download)\\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; semirook Q 10, rook Q 20, semibishop Q 01, anassa Q 11 ,3 and semiqueen Q 21. ... If Conjecture 2.4 is true, the four special partial queens of Theorem 2.3 will be the only ... For q= 3, a triangular conﬁguration of three pieces on the square board,\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA q-queens problem. VII. Combinatorial types of nonattacking chess riders (https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08981)\\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200